00:02:50.159 --> 00:02:55.800
Anyway, I was talking to Paolo Bacigalupi
and we had this great conversation about how

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.610
the formats that are available to us tend
to actually influence the stories that get

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:02.610
told.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.950
Like for ages the only video formats we had
were the thirty minute episode, the sixty

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.680
minute episode, and the movie length thing.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.440
But because YouTube happened, suddenly there
was this new format that was available to

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.340
people and this format wasn't influenced by
when advertising was gonna show up or just

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.599
by the weirdness of dividing a day into 24
pieces and saying, like, "Each one of these,

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:22.950
that's going to be the length of a TV episode".

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.290
And it was influenced just by what's the best
possible format for telling a quick story

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.380
or making a quick thing.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.760
And so suddenly in 2007 you have the Vlogbrothers
being, like, "We can never make a video more

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:33.760
than four minutes long.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.720
We're gonna impose that artificial restriction
on ourselves" just like we did when we decided

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.650
as a culture that days were gonna have 24
hunks.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:44.739
It's all arbitrary and it's weird and it's
great that we have stuck with that and, like,

00:03:44.739 --> 00:03:45.750
this is the format.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:50.080
And now that I have to say, despite the fact
that I only just got to the point, I have

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.319
to end this video because as I said, we imposed
an arbitrary distinction upon ourselves and

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:56.659
we must enforce it.

00:03:56.659 --> 00:03:58.209
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

